1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,915
ModelMint - Feature Walkthrough
Welcome to ModelMint, a secure model as a service platform. In this walkthrough, we will
upload a trained model, serve single and batch predictions, create scoped API keys, promote
new versions with canary traffic, detect input drift, and monitor every request end to end.

2
00:00:17,915 --> 00:00:28,395
01 - Model Registry
This is the ModelMint registry. It is empty right now, ready for the first model upload.
From here you can search, filter, inspect, and delete models.

3
00:00:28,395 --> 00:00:38,380
02 - Scoped API Keys (Locked)
The API keys page starts locked. Administrative actions such as creating or revoking keys
require an admin key, so access is never open by default.

4
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:48,120
03 - Admin Unlock
Unlocking with the bootstrap admin key reveals scoped key management, per-key usage, and the
security audit log. The key stays in this browser tab only.

5
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:58,935
04 - Creating a Scoped Key
Now we create a scoped key for a client. The plaintext secret is shown exactly once, and
only an HMAC hash is stored on the server. If it is lost, you rotate it.

6
00:00:58,935 --> 00:01:08,545
05 - Key Scopes and Audit
The client key has read, write, and monitoring scopes, plus predict. The audit log
immediately records the key creation with the exact scopes granted.

7
00:01:08,545 --> 00:01:22,150
06 - Upload and Sandbox Validation
Next, upload a scikit-learn pickle. ModelMint detects the framework, validates the artifact
in a sandbox, stores a SHA-256 checksum, and automatically creates a live inference
endpoint.

8
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:34,705
07 - Detected Schema
The model detail view shows the detected framework, task type, schema confidence, feature
count, class count, and sandbox load time. A synthetic input test confirms the model is
servable.

9
00:01:34,705 --> 00:01:44,750
08 - Task-Aware Try It
The Try It panel generates a task-aware example request. For a tabular model it sends a
features array, so you can test the endpoint without writing any code.

10
00:01:44,750 --> 00:01:53,955
09 - Single Prediction
A single prediction returns in milliseconds from a persistent sandboxed worker. The API
process never loads untrusted model code directly.

11
00:01:53,955 --> 00:02:02,990
10 - Batch Prediction
Batch mode accepts an array of instances and returns one prediction per row. This is useful
for scoring small datasets in a single request.

12
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:10,920
11 - Copy-Ready Integrations
Copy-ready curl and Python snippets make integration immediate. They use your API key and
the model's generated endpoint.

13
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:21,565
12 - Monitoring and Metrics
Monitoring aggregates live request counts, success rate, average and p95 latency, a latency
chart, and the most recent inference logs.

14
00:02:21,565 --> 00:02:31,925
13 - Upload a New Version
Upload a new version into the same model family. ModelMint validates it in the sandbox and
keeps it inactive, so production traffic is untouched until promotion.

15
00:02:31,925 --> 00:02:41,075
14 - Promote a Release
Promoting version two makes it the active release. Version one remains servable, so a
rollback is one click away if the new release misbehaves.

16
00:02:41,075 --> 00:02:49,690
15 - A/B Traffic Split
Traffic weights turn a release into a canary. Here version two and version one each receive
half of the requests through the stable family endpoint.

17
00:02:49,690 --> 00:02:59,860
16 - Input Drift Detection
Drift detection compares the recent request window with the baseline and flags features
whose distribution has shifted, without ever storing raw request bodies.

18
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:10,150
17 - Supported Formats API
The formats endpoint reports every supported framework, its file extensions, task types, and
whether its optional package is installed in this deployment.

19
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:21,255
18 - Prometheus Metrics
Prometheus metrics expose model counts by status, inference requests, latency totals, and
active API keys, ready to scrape into your observability stack.

20
00:03:21,255 --> 00:03:31,010
19 - Authentication Required
Security is enforced at the API layer. Calling predict without an API key is rejected with
401 Unauthorized, before any model code runs.

21
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:43,370
20 - Security Audit Log
The audit log tracks key creation, model uploads, promotions, rotations, revocations, and
deletions. Every security-sensitive action is attributable and timestamped.

22
00:03:43,370 --> 00:03:53,485
21 - Key Rotation
Rotating a key immediately invalidates the old secret and issues a new plaintext key, shown
once. Existing integrations must switch to the new value.

23
00:03:53,485 --> 00:04:01,280
22 - Key Revocation
Revoking a key disables it permanently. The key remains in the table with a revoked status,
preserving history for audits.

24
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:10,445
23 - Model Deletion
Deleting a model removes its stored artifact and inference endpoint. The registry returns to
an empty state, ready for the next upload.

25
00:04:10,445 --> 00:04:19,740
24 - Final Audit Trail
Every action remains visible in the audit trail. ModelMint is ready to mint, serve, and
govern models with scoped access and full observability.

26
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:35,355
Mint. Serve. Govern.
That is ModelMint. Upload a supported model, serve it instantly, promote new versions with
canary traffic, watch for input drift, and govern every action with scoped keys, sandboxed
execution, and a complete audit trail. Thanks for watching.

